ur.\n\nSimilarly, **F7**? F7=X would give F7–F8 = vertical two, and F7–G8–H9 diagonal? F7, G8, H9: if H9 were X, but H10=O.\n\nNot great.\n\nWhat about **H7**?\n\nH7=X would give:\n- H7–H8 vertical two\n- G7–H7–I7 row 7: G7=X, H7=X, I7=empty, J7=empty, K7=O\n  So if we play H7, and later I7 and J7, we could get four, but K7=O blocks.\n\nSo max four, but blocked.\n\nBut if we play **I7**, then:\n- I7–I8 vertical two, but I9=O → blocked.\n- Row 7: G7=X, H7=?, I7=X, J7=?, K7=O — could aim for G7–H7–I7–J7, but again blocked by K7.\n\nStill, **G9** seems better because it creates a vertical three with growth potential.\n\nBut wait — is there a **fork**?\n\nConsider **F9**:\n- F9=X: connects to F8=X → vertical two.\n- Diagonally: E10, F9, G8, H7, I6 — G8=X, F9=X, H7=?, E10=?, I6=?\n  If H7=X, then F9–G8–H7 = diagonal? Wait, direction: from I6 to E10: I6, H7, G8, F9, E10\n  So I6–H7–G8–F9–E10\n\nCurrently:\n- I6: empty\n- H7: empty\n- G8: X\n- F9: ?\n- E10: empty\n\nIf we play **F9=X**, and later H7=X, I6=X, then we could have a diagonal.\n\nBut not immediate.\n\nAlternatively, play **H7** to start building on row 7.\n\nBut still.\n\nWait — what about **G9** vs **H7**?\n\nG9: creates vertical three in G-column: G7, G8, G9 all X. That’s strong. Open at both ends. Can grow to four easily.\n\nH7: creates horizontal possibility on row 7 and vertical on H.\n\nBut row 7 has G7 and H7, but needs I7 and J7 — J7 is playable, but K7=O blocks the fifth.\n\nSo at best, four in a row on row 7: G7-H7-I7-J7, but K7=O prevents five unless we go left, but A7 to F7 are empty, so no.\n\nWait, no — row 7: A7 to O7\nOnly G7=X, K7=O\n\nSo if we play H7, I7, J7, we get G7-H7-I7-J7 = four, but J7 is before K7, so it’s four ending at J7, but no fifth because K7=O.\n\nSo maximum four, blocked.\n\nWhereas G9 gives a central vertical three, open on both ends.\n\nSimilarly, what about **G6**? G6=X would give G6-G7-G8 = three upward.\n\nSame value.\n\nBut G9 is symmetric.\n\nBut is there a difference?\